    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1798">

                <pb facs="1179" n="1063" xml:id="pg1063"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 823.00/121.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Minister McMillin to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Lima</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-02-08">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 8, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>First Vice President has been out of Peru for many months; Second Vice President
                    has resigned, leaving no official of old government to assume or claim the right
                    to executive power in the new. No other change in the situation since my last.
                    The question of recognition is now being raised by other legations and am
                    informed that in certain cases only they await American action. Papal Legate and
                    Italian, Brazilian and Argentine Ministers favor recognition. French Minister
                    undecided. No evidence of organized opposition to present régime, nor does any
                    seem probable. Believe ultimate recognition inevitable. Request instruction.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">McMillin</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
